      "question": {
        ":question/id": "f2c94a3b-95ff-5160-a594-ad4b98992511",
        ":question/problem": "What is the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=2x-5cos\u2061x$ at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is given by the value of the derivative ${y}^{\u2032}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos\u2061x)=-sin\u2061x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-5cos\u2061x) \\\\  & =2-5(-sin\u2061x) \\\\  & =2+5sin\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =2+5sin\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2+5(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate of change is $\\frac{9}{2}.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
