        ":question/id": "7af3519e-f7d3-5ca4-a6bd-1048972ce066",
        ":question/problem": "Find the derivative of $y=- 16 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{8}{x^{2}}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin x)=\\cos x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas and the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- 16 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + \\frac{8}{x^{2}}) \\\\ &= (16 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(- \\frac{16}{x^{3}}) \\\\ &= 16 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\frac{16}{x^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
