        ":question/problem": "What is the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=16 x - 40 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ at the point $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$?",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The slope is the derivative evaluated at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin x)=\\cos x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas and the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16 x - 40 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &= (40 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(16) \\\\ &= 40 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\ny^{\\prime}(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=16+40\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{6})=16+40\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $36.$",
